(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-viral-and-fungal-infections-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Viral and Fungal Infections Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 70-year-old with obesity, diabetes and incontinence is examined for candidal skin disease. Which sites does the lecture list as the common ones to inspect?","choices":["Inframammary, axillary, abdominal, inguinal, and interdigital folds","The scalp, the beard area, the eyebrows, and the nasolabial folds","The trunk, the neck, and the proximal arms","The palms, the soles, and the periungual skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the perineal folds are on the list too, making six named sites. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has an itchy, sharply demarcated plaque on the proximal medial thigh involving the crural fold. The scrotum is spared. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea cruris","Candidal intertrigo","Erythrasma","Tinea corporis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — sparing of the scrotum is the key discriminator. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 26-year-old has an itchy hand eruption and you suspect an id reaction. Which three criteria does the lecture give for establishing it?","choices":["A dermatophyte infection elsewhere, presence of fungal elements at the reaction site, and resolution when a corticosteroid is applied","A bacterial infection elsewhere, absence of fungal elements at the reaction site, and resolution when an antibiotic is given","A positive Wood lamp at the reaction site, a negative culture elsewhere, and resolution within 24 hours of treatment","A dermatophyte infection elsewhere, absence of fungal elements at the reaction site, and resolution when the primary infection is treated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three must be satisfied. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 22-year-old wrestler has a single round, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous dry scaly plaque on his forearm. The centre has begun to clear, leaving a ring. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Fixed drug eruption","Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Discoid lupus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — progressive central clearing gives the annular \"ringworm\" outline. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 78-year-old had a thoracic zoster rash four months ago. The rash healed, but she has constant burning pain in the same area and cannot bear her shirt touching the skin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Postherpetic neuralgia","Zoster sine herpete","Recurrent herpes zoster in the same dermatome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset, with allodynia. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A patient with forehead zoster has no lesions on the nose. What does that tell you about his risk of eye involvement?","choices":["Absence of the Hutchinson sign confirms involvement of a different cranial nerve","Absence of the Hutchinson sign does not exclude eye involvement","Absence of the Hutchinson sign reliably excludes eye involvement","Absence of the Hutchinson sign means ophthalmology review can wait two weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a negative sign is not reassuring. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A patient has a single 3-centimetre annular plaque on the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment, and how should it be applied?","choices":["Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole, applied only to the cleared centre of the lesion","An oral antifungal from the outset, applied in every patient with a single localized plaque","A combination corticosteroid and antifungal product, applied to the lesion and well beyond its border","Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole, applied to the lesion and 1 to 2 centimetres beyond its border"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treating beyond the border covers the advancing edge. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient cannot take terbinafine for confirmed onychomycosis. Which alternative does the lecture name, and what does it say about fluconazole?","choices":["Griseofulvin is the alternative; fluconazole is first-line in the United States","Nystatin is the alternative; fluconazole is contraindicated in nail disease","Itraconazole is an alternative; fluconazole is off label in the United States","Fluconazole is the alternative; itraconazole is off label in the United States"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both points come from the same slide. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"The parents of a child with tinea capitis ask how long fungal particles survive on the hairbrush she shares with her sister. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Fungal particles remain viable for a few hours, so normal washing of the brush between uses is enough","Fungal particles cannot survive off the scalp at all, so shared items carry no risk of transmission","Fungal particles remain viable for months, but only on furniture and clothing rather than on combs","Fungal particles remain viable for months, so hair-care items must not be shared and should be cleaned"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck emphasises months of viability. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 23-year-old has finely scaling tan macules on the trunk, and you perform potassium hydroxide microscopy on a scale from one lesion. Which finding would you expect?","choices":["Multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions","Short hyphae with clusters of yeast, described as \"spaghetti and meatballs\"","Long branching septate hyphae without any yeast forms present","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae without any short hyphal fragments"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the classic appearance. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A patient with varicella is admitted to hospital. Which precautions are required?","choices":["Standard, airborne, and contact precautions","Contact precautions alone, with no airborne component","Droplet precautions alone","Standard precautions alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — all three are required. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A patient has recurrent, extensive candidal intertrigo. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Evaluate for diabetes and immunosuppression","Repeat the same topical course for a further six months","Switch to a systemic corticosteroid to control the inflammation","Refer for surgical excision of the affected skin folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrent or extensive disease prompts this. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A patient with recurrent genital herpes simplex asks how to reduce the risk of infecting his partner. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Neither suppressive antivirals nor condoms affect transmission at all, so the only option available is to avoid sexual contact permanently","Suppressive valacyclovir lowers transmission and condoms reduce risk, but neither eliminates it; avoid contact during the prodrome and while lesions are active","Suppressive valacyclovir eliminates transmission entirely, so no other precaution is needed once it is started","Condoms eliminate transmission entirely, so antiviral therapy adds nothing for a patient who uses them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — risk is reduced, never abolished. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"You are about to prescribe a systemic antifungal for an 8-year-old with confirmed tinea capitis. Which safety step does the lecture identify before the first dose?","choices":["Review drug interactions and cardiac disease, and obtain a baseline electrocardiogram in every patient before the first dose","No safety step is needed, because oral antifungals used for scalp disease carry no clinically important interactions","Review drug interactions and hepatic disease, and obtain baseline liver tests when indicated by the agent, its labeling, and patient risk","Review drug interactions and renal disease, and obtain a baseline creatinine in every patient regardless of the agent chosen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — baseline testing is indication-driven, not automatic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient with candidal intertrigo has marked inflammation. What does the lecture say about adding a corticosteroid?","choices":["Use a high-potency corticosteroid for several weeks, alongside adequate antifungal treatment","Use a low-potency corticosteroid as the sole treatment until the inflammation settles","Never use a corticosteroid in any circumstance, because it will always cause tinea incognito","Consider a low-potency corticosteroid only briefly, and only alongside adequate antifungal treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — brief, low-potency, and never alone. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A patient with a few small asymptomatic common warts asks for aggressive freezing to remove them quickly. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Aggressive freezing is appropriate, because it eradicates the virus and prevents recurrence","Aggressive freezing is appropriate, because common warts do not resolve without treatment","Avoid freezing entirely, because cryotherapy is never an appropriate wart treatment","Avoid excessive freezing or destructive therapy for benign lesions likely to resolve spontaneously"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — weigh the harms against a self-limiting lesion. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A patient has a confirmed id reaction on the hands secondary to tinea pedis. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Treat the primary dermatophyte infection, since the id reaction resolves when it is treated","Treat the hand eruption with a topical antifungal, since fungus is present at both sites","Treat the hand eruption with an oral antibiotic, since the reaction is bacterial in origin","Observe both sites without treatment, since the reaction and the primary infection both self-resolve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distant eruption follows the primary site. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A patient has a lesion strongly suggestive of tinea corporis, but the potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the identical potassium hydroxide preparation weekly until it eventually turns positive","Start an oral antifungal without further testing, since the clinical picture alone is sufficient","Obtain a fungal culture, since high clinical suspicion with a negative preparation is an indication for it","Accept the negative result and treat the lesion as nummular eczema with a topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — culture is for exactly this situation, and for refractory cases. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has extensive giant molluscum lesions across the face. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Excise every lesion surgically under general anaesthesia at the first visit","Start an oral antifungal, since extensive facial lesions suggest a dermatophyte","Evaluate for immunosuppression, including human immunodeficiency virus when appropriate","Reassure him that facial lesions resolve within weeks and arrange no follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — extensive or atypical disease raises this question. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A patient is diagnosed with tinea incognito. Which is the most appropriate next step, and what should you warn her about?","choices":["Continue the corticosteroid while adding an antifungal; warn that the eruption may worsen if the steroid is stopped abruptly","Stop the corticosteroid and obtain testing from the cleared centre; warn that the eruption should settle immediately","Increase the corticosteroid potency and re-test in six weeks; warn that testing is unreliable during treatment","Stop the corticosteroid or calcineurin inhibitor and obtain testing from an active edge; warn that inflammation may rebound after withdrawal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the rebound is expected and should be anticipated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"The parents of a child with molluscum contagiosum want it treated immediately. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Immediate oral antiviral therapy is required, because the lesions will otherwise spread to the whole family","Observation is appropriate for many patients, because most clear spontaneously and procedures may blister, pigment, or scar","Immediate destruction of every lesion is required, because untreated molluscum progresses to malignancy","Observation is appropriate, because the lesions always resolve within two to three weeks without treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — although resolution may take months to years. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"A patient's intertriginous rash has become malodorous with erosions and drainage. Which does this raise concern for?","choices":["An id reaction at a distant site","Tinea incognito from prior steroid use","Malignant transformation of the plaque","Bacterial coinfection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — malodor, erosion or drainage point beyond Candida. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A parent asks when her child with varicella may return to normal activities. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["The child is contagious from 1 to 2 days before the rash until all lesions have crusted","The child is contagious only from the day the rash appears until the fever resolves","The child is contagious for 21 days from exposure regardless of the lesions","The child is contagious only while new vesicles are forming, not once any have crusted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — contagiousness begins before the rash is visible. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A patient with acute herpes zoster asks how long he remains infectious. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["For 21 days from the onset of the rash","He was never infectious at any point","Until the lesions have dried","Until the pain has resolved completely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — infectious until the lesions dry. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 30-year-old runner has a painful plantar wart on the ball of the foot. Which treatments does the lecture give?","choices":["Salicylic acid 40% or cryotherapy","Cantharidin 0.7% or curettage","Berdazimer 10.3% gel or a topical retinoid","Oral terbinafine or topical ciclopirox"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the two named options for plantar warts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is diagnosed with tinea barbae. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid to settle the inflammation first, followed by an oral antifungal after two weeks","Oral antifungal therapy with griseofulvin or terbinafine, plus shaving or hair removal and warm compresses to remove crusts","Topical antifungal cream alone applied twice daily to the beard area, plus shaving or hair removal and warm compresses to remove crusts","Oral antibiotic therapy with a first-generation cephalosporin, plus shaving and warm compresses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — topicals do not penetrate the follicle. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient has pityriasis versicolor over the upper trunk. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral terbinafine, given for six weeks because it concentrates well in sweat","Topical nystatin, applied twice daily until the scale has fully resolved","Topical therapy, such as ketoconazole shampoo or cream, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox, or topical terbinafine","Oral therapy, such as fluconazole or itraconazole, given to every patient at presentation whatever the extent of disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is first-line. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Before prescribing for an 84-year-old with postherpetic neuralgia, which factors does the lecture say to individualize for?","choices":["Immune status, vaccination history, and prior antiviral use","Kidney function, falls, anticholinergic burden, and drug interactions","Hepatic function, weight, dietary habits, and smoking history","Cardiac function, blood pressure, exercise tolerance, and diet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four named considerations. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A patient has recurrent tinea pedis despite repeated topical courses, and also has thickened discoloured toenails. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add a topical corticosteroid between antifungal courses to reduce the inflammation","Stop all treatment and observe, since tinea pedis resolves spontaneously in most adults","Consider oral therapy, treat the coexisting onychomycosis, and reinforce moisture control","Continue the same topical course indefinitely, since recurrence is expected and untreatable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an untreated nail reservoir drives recurrence. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which self-care measure does the lecture single out as essential for a patient with tinea pedis?","choices":["Drying between the toes after bathing","Wearing occlusive footwear throughout the day","Soaking the feet in hot water each evening","Applying a corticosteroid powder to the shoes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags this one as essential. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
